[
    {
        "content": "<p>I am a little bit confused about the \"asserted facts\" section in the grind error messages: What counts as an asserted fact? It doesn't seem to be just the hypotheses and conclusion but it also isn't identical to the True equivalence class. Does it additionally consider things found through e-matching?<br>\n(This is for a talk in a class taught by <span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"634338\">Michael Rothgang</span>  but he encouraged me to ask here.)</p>",
        "id": 608832824,
        "sender_full_name": "Hannah Scholz",
        "timestamp": 1783456901
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(I don't know the answer to Hannah's question myself: so count me interested in the answer also.)</p>",
        "id": 608834222,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1783457454
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The things you see in the asserted facts can come from a lot of sources, the best approximation would be: hypotheses, e-matching instances, case splits (including mbtc based case splits), and facts dreamed up by theory solvers that they deemd important enough to communicate to the rest of the system (though the theory solvers also have other means to communicate their information, e.g. by combining equivalence classes directly). </p>\n<p>The <code>True</code> equivalence class does not only contain the asserted facts but also some derived ones through congruence closure, propagators, and potentially also theory solvers. Consider:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∨</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">¬</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">False</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">grind</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Here we get:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">grind</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Goal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">diagnostics</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">▼</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">facts</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Asserted</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">facts</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">▼</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">prop</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∨</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">prop</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">¬</span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">eqc</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">propositions</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">▼</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">prop</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">prop</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∨</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">eqc</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">False</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">propositions</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">▼</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">prop</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>grind asserted the assumptions as facts and then triggered an internal propagator that realized that <code>b</code> must also be true and added that to the equivalence class directly.</p>",
        "id": 608844676,
        "sender_full_name": "Henrik Böving",
        "timestamp": 1783461849
    },
